Market Challenges
Heat and Moisture Performance Limitations
PLA's thermal and moisture-related properties can restrict its use in applications requiring high-temperature resistance or demanding barrier performance. Manufacturers must select suitable grades, additives and product designs to meet specific requirements. These technical limitations can make substitution more difficult in applications where conventional polymers offer established performance advantages.

Composting and Waste Management Infrastructure
Industrial compostability depends on suitable processing conditions and certified product design. Collection systems and industrial composting facilities are not equally available across regions. If PLA packaging enters an incompatible waste stream, its intended end-of-life benefits may not be realized.

Market Restraints
Competition from Conventional Plastics
Polyethylene, polypropylene and other established polymers benefit from mature production capacity, broad availability and competitive pricing. PLA producers must demonstrate suitable performance and acceptable economics to encourage adoption, especially in high-volume packaging markets.

Feedstock and Lactide Supply Volatility
PLA manufacturing depends on agricultural carbohydrates and intermediate chemical production. Changes in crop availability, feedstock prices and lactide supply can affect manufacturing economics. Producers must maintain reliable sourcing and consistent polymer quality to meet customer requirements.

